  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "This specimen gives a strong structural answer to carrier independence: carriers only move deterministic signed CBOR envelope bytes, while feed and chunk meaning stays behind pcid dispatch or wrapper protocols. It is still only a partial scenario fit because the actual chunk-feed replication semantics are not defined in this envelope spec.",
    "strengths": [
      "The same signed envelope bytes can travel over different carriers without changing interpretation.",
      "pcid and sig_pcid cleanly separate payload semantics, signature scheme, and transport concerns.",
      "Unknown-pCID store-and-forward with explicit unsupported-pCID failure supports sparse knowledge and visible boundary failures."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "Chunk identity, ordering, and replication semantics are deferred to an unspecified payload protocol.",
      "Opaque payloads reduce human and LLM auditability when handlers are unavailable.",
      "The specimen is still draft and unfrozen, so long-term stability is not yet demonstrated."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Teams could mistake a carrier-neutral envelope for a complete carrier-independent feed design.",
      "Handler fragmentation could produce widely replicated but uninterpretable content.",
      "Wrapper or relay patterns may diverge if hop-local evidence is not standardized."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What payload protocol makes feed and chunk semantics carrier-independent across UDP, git, libp2p, IPFS, and similar carriers?",
      "Should relay or hop-local evidence live in wrapper envelopes, payload records, or separate accounting objects?",
      "Is the current signature coverage sufficient when replicated content gains wrappers across different carriers?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
